@mvadari 

What are you thoughts here with testNestedMultiSign?
It seems like this disables the creation of invalid nested signer lists, and then allows the master key to be used, regardless of why it was disabled, in the case of invalid signer lists. That does seem a relatively elegant lesser of two evils and will likely unstick some folk, but what are your thoughts on actual nested multisign use cases? Do you believe they are worth supporting? I suppose the people who found themselves in such a pickle had reasons to try nesting.

```suggestion
    // the delegate account itself can not be among the signers.
```

```suggestion
        // For simulate, skip signature validation when neither SigningPubKey nor Signers are provided
```

It looks like the `setup_tx` lambda is effectively a `Json::Value` used as initialiser.
If so, consider making it a  `Json::Value` rather than a lambda.
I like the idea to use an immediately-invoked lambda to directly initialise it.
That way, it could be made const  `Json::Value` (though const is not needed here):

``` C++
Json::Value const setup_tx = [] {
     Json::Value jv;
     jv[jss::tx_json][jss::Account] = Account("alice").human();
     ...
     return jv;
}();
```

Account alice is expanded to avoid need to capture.
Also removed parentheses and trailing return fwiw.

